why 2 0 DOUBLING Soprano (G–A) and tenor (B–A) meet on A by step from opposite sides.
why 3 3/4 CROSSING The bass rises to B♭, the seventh of C⁷ (V of IV), while the tenor dips to G under it. Then the bass leaps down to E, back below the tenor.
why 3 7/8 CROSSING The tenor climbs back G–A–B♭ under the bass's held B♭; on the next beat the bass drops to E.
why 5 0 DOUBLING The tenor's B♭, a seventh, falls to A while the tune leaps up to A. Both land on A: a correct resolution beats the ideal doubling.
why 5 3/4 DOUBLING Soprano and alto share B on V. The soprano's B rises to C as it should; the alto's falls to A, the root of the deceptive vi.
why 6 0 SPACING At the deceptive cadence the alto drops to a low A while the soprano rises to C. The gap lasts an eighth: the alto climbs to E right after.
why 6 0 LEADING_TONE A deceptive cadence: V moves to vi. The soprano's B rises to C, so the alto's B falls to A and doubles the root of vi, the usual choice.
why 6 1/8 SPACING The tenor drops to a low C while the alto leaps up to E; the voices regroup within the A minor chord before the next beat.
why 6 1/8 LOW_SPACING The tenor drops to C, a third above the bass's low A, for only an eighth; then it leaps up an octave and the chord opens out again.
why 7 7/8 DOUBLING Tenor and bass both reach B on a weak eighth. The tenor's B rises to C; the bass's B is a passing note in its walk down C–B–A.
why 8 1/4 CROSSING In the final cadence the tenor drops to a low D under the bass's G as the bass leaps up and then an octave down. The voices trade places for an eighth to reach the last chord.
why 8 1/4 LEAPS The tenor falls C–A–D, outlining the cadence chords on its way down to the low register of the final chord.
